8 Linux Command Mistakes I See Developers Make All the Time.

Give it a read 👇

[1.] 𝐒𝐩𝐚𝐜𝐞𝐬 𝐌𝐚𝐭𝐭𝐞𝐫
Unlike some operating systems, spaces in filenames and paths must be properly escaped or quoted in Linux.

Forgetting this leads to "file not found" errors and confusion.

Example:
cd My Documents ❌

It needs to be -
cd 'My Documents' ✅
or
cd My\ Documents ✅

------------

[2.] 𝐂𝐚𝐬𝐞 𝐒𝐞𝐧𝐬𝐢𝐭𝐢𝐯𝐢𝐭𝐲
Linux is case-sensitive.

'myfile.txt' is different from 'MyFile.txt'.

Tab completion can help avoid mistakes, but awareness is key.

------------

[3.] 𝐇𝐢𝐝𝐝𝐞𝐧 𝐅𝐢𝐥𝐞𝐬
Files and directories starting with a '.' are hidden by default.

Use 'ls -a' to reveal them and be cautious when working with them – they might contain important configurations.

------------

[4.] 𝐫𝐦 -𝐫𝐟
Recursive forced deletion.

While essential for cleanup, a typo using this in the wrong directory can be disastrous.

Always double-check your targets before executing.

------------

[5.] 𝐎𝐮𝐭𝐩𝐮𝐭 𝐑𝐞𝐝𝐢𝐫𝐞𝐜𝐭𝐢𝐨𝐧
'>' overwrites an existing file.
'>>' appends.

Using '>' when you mean '>>' can wipe out data.

Consider using 'noclobber' mode (using 'set -o noclobber') to help prevent accidental overwrites.

------------

[6.] '𝐜𝐩' 𝐚𝐧𝐝 '𝐦𝐯' 𝐖𝐢𝐭𝐡𝐨𝐮𝐭 '-𝐢'
These commands silently overwrite existing files.

Use the '-i' flag for an interactive prompt or '-n' to never overwrite.

------------

[7.] '𝐜𝐡𝐦𝐨𝐝' 𝐚𝐧𝐝 𝐎𝐜𝐭𝐚𝐥 𝐏𝐞𝐫𝐦𝐢𝐬𝐬𝐢𝐨𝐧𝐬
Understand that 'chmod 755' is not the same as 'chmod +755'.

The first sets absolute permissions, the latter modifies them.

Know your octal permission values!

------------

[8.] 𝐁𝐚𝐜𝐤𝐠𝐫𝐨𝐮𝐧𝐝 𝐉𝐨𝐛𝐬 𝐚𝐧𝐝 '𝐧𝐨𝐡𝐮𝐩'
Background jobs (using '&') get terminated when the shell session ends.

Use 'nohup' or job control tools like 'screen' or 'tmux' for long-running processes you wish to persist.
